  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nMid-level ridging remains overhead early this morning with energy \nrounding the apex of the ridge sparking convection across the High \nPlains. This activity will slowly move southeast and could lead to \nsome showers across north central Kansas, but should largely \ndissipate before reaching the forecast area. Cloud cover and light \neast-southeast surface winds near a weak backdoor cold front will \nlimit warming to some degree, but still expect temperatures to heat \nup into the mid and upper 90s with peak heat index values around 100-\n105 degrees. An uncapped and unstable airmass this afternoon will \nsupport scattered thunderstorm development, mainly along a \nconvergence axis across eastern Kansas. Strong to severe storms are \npossible given 20-25kts of effective shear and SBCAPE of 1500-2000 \nJ/kg. Inverted-V soundings and DCAPE of 1100-1300 J/kg favor \ndamaging wind gusts around 60 MPH as the primary hazard, with hail \nup to quarter size being a lower probability threat. Most likely \ntiming for initiation is 2-4 PM with storms continuing into the \nevening hours before dissipating. There is still some potential for \na complex that develops north of the forecast area to move into \nKansas during the evening. CAMs have mixed ideas on the location of \nthis complex with the majority keeping this round east of the \nforecast area. These storms could produce damaging wind gusts if \nthey do impact far northeast Kansas. \n\nUncertainty remains in temperatures on Monday as cloud cover and \nprecipitation may linger through the morning with the potential for \na convective cold pool to move through the area as well. \nTemperatures may be slower to warm up, especially across far \nnortheast Kansas, but there is good consensus in a southerly wind \nworking to overcome any outflow-cooled air and bump temperatures \nback into the 90s by Monday afternoon. There remains a low (<10%) \nchance for a shower or storm to develop Monday afternoon, but a lack \nof focused ascent favors mostly dry conditions. Temperatures and \nheat indices are more marginal for an Excessive Heat Warning today \nand Monday across northern portions of the area, but heat index \nvalues will still reach near 100 degrees and the prolonged duration \nof the current heat wave leads to an increased risk of heat-related \nillnesses. As such, have not make any changes to the Extreme Heat \nWarning.\n\nA pattern change is still progged to occur Tuesday into Wednesday as \na potent shortwave ejects east across the Northern Plains towards \nthe Great Lakes, shunting a cold front into the central CONUS. Ahead \nof the front, southwesterly winds will boost temperatures into the \nupper 90s to around 100 degrees again Tuesday afternoon with heat \nindex values of 100-108 degrees. As the front moves into the \nforecast area Tuesday evening and night, showers and storms are \nexpected to develop along the boundary and slowly translate south \ninto Wednesday. The overall severe risk is low, but there could be \nsome hail or gusty winds within this activity. The front hangs up \nsomewhere in the region through the end of the week, leading to \ncontinued chances for showers and storms. Cooler temperatures build \ninto the area behind the front with highs in the 80s on Thursday, \nwarming back into the upper 80s and low 90s to end the week.",
